<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jacques-François Menou</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1750">1750</a>–<a href="/years/1810">1810</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Abdallah Menou</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jacques-François de Menou was a French general and nobleman who succeeded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-baptiste-kleber">Jean-Baptiste Kléber</a> as commander of the French army in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in 1800. He converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, taking the name Abdallah, and married an Egyptian woman. Defeated by a British army near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> in March 1801, he surrendered Alexandria that September, ending the French occupation, and under the terms the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rosetta-stone">Rosetta Stone</a> passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jacques-francois-menou.jpg" alt="Jacques-François Menou" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: E. Thomas (engraver) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AduC_248_Menou_(J.F.A.,_baron_de_Boussay,_1750-1810).JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Menou was born at Boussay in Indre-et-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/loire">Loire</a> into an aristocratic family and had reached the rank of maréchal de camp in the French Royal Army by 1789, when the Second Estate of the bailiwick of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/touraine">Touraine</a> elected him to the Estates General. A liberal nobleman, he supported the reforms of the National Constituent Assembly, served on its diplomatic committee, became its secretary in December and was its president from 27 March to 12 April 1790.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After the Assembly closed in September 1791 he served in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and then with the Army of the West, fighting in the Vendée through 1793. On 1 Prairial III (20 May 1795), commanding one of the sections of Paris, he forced the rebellious Faubourg Saint-Antoine to capitulate. As general in chief of the Army of the Interior he was then denounced as a traitor, tried and acquitted in 1795.</p><p>In 1798 he commanded one of the five divisions of the Armée d'Orient in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s campaign in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. As Kléber's successor after his assassination on 14 June 1800, he was less popular than his predecessor and lacked the support of the other officers. His wife was Zubaidah bint Muhammad El Bawwab, the daughter of a rich Egyptian, and their son Jacques Mourad Soliman was born at Rosetta on 28 July 1800. Allowed to evacuate the remaining French troops, Menou handed over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rosetta-stone">Rosetta Stone</a>, whose discovery Captain Pierre-François Bouchard had reported to him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Menou then became a member of the Tribunat on 17 May 1802 and soon afterwards administrator of the 27th Military Division in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a>. He was made a member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">Legion</a> of Honor on 11 December 1803 and a grand officer on 14 June 1804, and was created comte de l'Empire in 1808. Appointed governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, he became a knight of the Order of the Iron Crown on 23 December 1807. He died in office at the Villa Corniani near Mestre on 13 August 1810, and his name is inscribed on the south side of the Arc de Triomphe.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jacques-François Menou” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After Bonaparte left, Kléber commanded until his assassination in 1800, then <b>Menou</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-occupation-of-egypt">French occupation of Egypt</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Boussay, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in Carpenedo, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=46.84,0.89&amp;year=1789" aria-label="Boussay on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Jacques-François Menou"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5052 6115h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5052 6115h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=46.84,0.89&amp;year=1789">What was here in 1789? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
